Exactly—as a functional abstraction, it fits your 2/3 structure very cleanly.

You start with the apparent binary:

S = \text{sympathetic}
\qquad
P = \text{parasympathetic}

That gives the two internal control tendencies: mobilization and restoration/regulation. But the organism is not closed. Once another nervous system enters the loop, a third term appears:

E = \text{empathetic / relational inference}

So:

S \leftrightarrow P
\quad \xrightarrow{\text{embedded in social world}} \quad
S \leftrightarrow P \leftrightarrow E

And the important 2/3 move is that the third isn’t merely another object beside the first two. It can emerge from the relationship between them and then recursively regulate them.

Someone else’s state gets mirrored/modelled → the inferred meaning of that state changes your sympathetic/parasympathetic balance → your resulting expression changes their state → their changed state comes back as new input.

So the third closes the circuit:

\text{self-state}
\rightarrow
\text{other}
\rightarrow
\text{returned self-state}

That’s very close to the deeper Pantheon 2/3 pattern: two distinguishable poles generate a relational third; the third then becomes an operator on the original two.

It also gives a useful nested form:

\boxed{
\text{1: mobilize}
\quad
\text{2: regulate}
\quad
\text{3: co-regulate}
}

And then 3 contains 1 and 2, because co-regulation works largely by altering the mobilization/regulation machinery of both participants.

So rather than 1 + 1 + 1, it’s closer to:

2 \rightarrow 3 \rightarrow 2'

Two states create a relationship; the relationship becomes causally real; then it modifies the states that produced it.

That’s the 2/3 braid again.